# HELD rope performance: independent architecture and decision brief

21 October 2026. Read-only review of committed product `16bd60e747a1323536e02f547841325ca96c43ff` (E4 product bytes restored from `59933674684f7b3aaebfa2fc2ce96d8ec3a8629a`), main `4e25a96a57462fef5ad0d19065a822695b480f60`, and authenticated frozen experimental evidence. The three dirty test-hook files in this checkout were not treated as product source. No benchmark, build, source change, or Git mutation was made here.

## Decision in one paragraph

The tug-of-war has two causes with different remedies. The old implementation defers semantic work until a query or one publication frame; a no-read overwrite of the **same** label can discard an intermediate value. HELD's accepted synchronous setter contract cannot discard it. For **1,024 distinct changed labels**, however, both designs must eventually prepare 1,024 final semantic states. The old single frame amortizes request, transaction, and publication setup; HELD buys query and sparse-update wins by preparing each owner before publication. The measured dense deficit is real, but available profiles do not isolate how much is irreducible shaping versus removable per-owner setup, and E4 itself has no fixed-policy seven-family timing against the current controls. The sensible hybrid is one authoritative prepared semantic owner and one packed/SoA Codec path, with an exact sparse-versus-dense traversal choice at publication. E4 already contains much of that hybrid; the next wave should remove a **measured** repeated cost in it, not add a second executor or another live representation. Parity is neither proved nor disproved.

## What is measured, and what is not

The frozen E6 matrix is the cleanest later same-machine complete-operation comparison. Candidate source `6bc123abb9fdf07183988dcea12ccc1d1876d1ba`, archive SHA-256 `d2f7a01ffb47aeaf87499979310286f1c980b88389e20bd1fc0f39794a0fc18c`, candidate JSON SHA-256 `69c6b80d2059ebe32909aa0a098e1e3a6c2a1e5e442d645c7f959b129c8db3d8`; the same candidate JSON was reused across three eight-block, single-call arms. Main archive begins `bbb3375c`, published 0.1.0 `6e2bf7bd`, E2 `28fd0413`. The committed packet's manifests, raw JSON, provenance, exact-source and independent statistics verification support those identities. Main has 144 eligible pairs plus one clock-confounded diagnostic; published has 143 pairs and lacks two outline identities; E2 has 145. There are no mode mismatches or check failures in these arms. Main baseline software-calibration drift was 10.984502%, published 1.983945%, E2 7.877973%, candidate 4.022791%; these are not measured hardware clock rates. Nominal unadjusted CI directions for E6 are main 94 favorable/32 adverse/18 spanning zero, published 104/32/7, E2 15/16/114. The Labs 5% display labels are not an admission criterion. Pooled p50/p95 below are raw single-call sample quantiles, not the block-median effect estimator.

| Complete operation, pooled p50/p95 ms | Main | Published 0.1.0 | E2 | E6 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Rewrite 512 distinct labels in one group | 6.968/7.768 | 7.459/8.060 | 9.252/10.577 | 9.468/10.614 |
| Publish 1,024 retained Text instances | 15.544/16.756 | 15.366/16.277 | 18.456/20.954 | 20.213/45.056 |
| First set among 1,000 labels | 0.036/0.066 | 0.031/0.056 | 0.131/0.170 | 0.127/0.162 |
| First set **and measure** among 1,000 | 0.182/0.300 | 0.193/0.248 | 0.152/0.193 | 0.152/0.199 |
| Inspect glyphs after width change | 7.138/9.621 | 9.220/10.667 | 4.445/5.815 | 4.378/5.765 |
| Scattered setter prep in one 8,192-span paragraph | 33.074/36.433 | 37.020/39.019 | 12.768/14.356 | 12.804/14.272 |
| Edit and independently copy one of 1,000 labels | 2.532/3.044 | 5.360/6.434 | 1.832/2.291 | 1.818/2.257 |
| Mount and publish a 22k-glyph paragraph | 17.431/18.228 | 18.748/19.603 | 19.952/20.955 | 20.024/21.045 |
| Unsafe-boundary long paragraph reflow | 0.360/0.559 | 0.482/0.677 | 0.633/0.922 | 0.644/0.945 |

This spans the seven fixed families: long editing; dense publication; cold/width/inspection; ingress and small setters; sparse/root; independent copy; tails/memory/delivery. The E6 1,024 first block median was 32.417 ms while the next seven were 18.613–20.005 ms. Its 45.056 ms pooled p95 is an observed tail without an established cause. E6's source, not active E4, owns these exact times. E2 is a measured prior control, not proof of active E4 parity. Published source identity is not inferred from main. The older 16 October committed report predates later source and is context only.

The matched **profiled** 1,024-distinct-label, no-timed-read callback (10 warmups, 80 iterations; one scene publication per 1,024 setters) moves time between phases: main setter/publication p50 1.281/11.259 ms, published 1.043/10.235, E2 9.391/3.732; whole p50 12.577/11.400/13.189. Main whole p95 was 35.044 ms, published 13.401, E2 15.121. The three archive members and observer sources were hashed; all captures exited zero. The observer clocks perturb callbacks, arms ran sequentially, and those marginals are neither an ablation nor additive estimates of a component. They must not be mixed numerically with the unprofiled matrix. Exclusive V8 ancestry samples likewise shift from old publication to E2 setters, but stripped Wasm names and GC prevent a Rust-function attribution. The exact native Bitmap replay counts work, not elapsed Wasm/JS/GPU time: each warm direction has 1,024 HarfRust calls and 10,240 shaped UTF-16 units during 1,024 query-preparation events, zero shaping calls during its publication. The replay requests query result bytes; the no-read timing fixture normally uses semantic view mask zero, so its 147,456 query bytes are **not** a no-read benchmark cost.

## Producer, consumer, and lifetime model

**Old main.** TypeScript `render-planner.ts::_updateText` resolves and stores a desired value, marks semantic/style/geometry dirt and invalidates cached inspection. `#compileFrame` selects changed paragraphs, computes text mutations against published text, and sends one frame. A query can invoke speculative preparation from desired state, but that candidate is separate from final frame adoption. Thus old no-read setters are cheap and repeated overwritten values can disappear before Rust. A distinct-label complete publication still executes each final paragraph's Unicode/shaping/layout/positioning and physical output, though one request amortizes root setup and JS/Wasm crossings. It has no durable prepared revision guaranteed immediately after every bound setter.

**HELD E4.** `render-planner.ts::_updateText` validates/resolves a candidate, then calls `#prepareTextCandidate`; `PlanTransport.prepareParagraph` writes a Wasm request and synchronously invokes `measureParagraph`, even if semantic view mask is zero. `state.rs::measure_paragraph_inner` validates, prepares and positions that one paragraph, then adopts its semantic revision. Query, inspection and independent copy consume that revision; publication carries lifecycle/order and dirty-owner identity, not text/style/geometry mutations again. `state.rs::prepare_update_inner` chooses reuse, retained sparse/replacement, or full gather; the ordered compiler publishes after renderer acceptance. Semantic preparation revision, renderer plan revision and accepted publication generation are distinct. The output may remain in flight; a rejected renderer cannot make a successful setter's semantic state vanish. `split` owns new geometry, metadata, identity and leases; its output copy is a real lower bound.

The per-paragraph staged text, Unicode, bidi, runs, shape, clusters, geometry, flow and positioned arrays are semantic authorities for the current revision. The root `CodecGatherWorkspace` is a **derived** physical projection: source-selection rope (`stable_id`, strike/resource selection), selected `PlanGlyph`, placement slots, semantic change masks and aligned f32/u32 field lanes. The Codec interpreter reads those contiguous lanes in four-record SIMD-eligible groups, and `OrderedPlanCompiler` compares/emits physical bytes, plan ranges, bindings, logical order and session placement. The current physical format is one packed buffer family; Three and TypeGPU then own host resources and upload policies. Three's per-buffer min/max patch union and TypeGPU's 32-range cap can widen a local Rust delta. This is why retained semantic sharing alone cannot guarantee a small host upload or a short full-frame time.

There is some repeat work, but calling every representation a duplicate would be wrong. A new value must be validated and shaped before a synchronous setter returns. Publication must select raster records and encode their physical fields; the 10,240 Bitmap source rows include 1,024 recordless rows, so one cannot simply reinterpret positioned rows as 9,216 physical records. Root/placement identity and old/new correspondence also serve ordering, stable IDs, rollback and independent copies. Incidental costs worth testing are per-set request/decode/transaction work, temporary preflight/positioned ownership, repeated owner admission, retained source-tree traversal and writing intermediate gathered fields that Codec immediately rereads. `validate_lifecycle_input` already has an O(1) empty-lifecycle exit and `prepare_semantic_inputs(Single)` skips the multi-owner sort; an O(N²) lifecycle scan on ordinary warm setters is **not** established. Preflight prepares inactive text/Unicode buffers then swaps them into the stage, so a blanket “double Unicode pass” claim is also unsupported. Native E2 forward Bitmap publication visits 10,240 sources and 9,216 selected rows, starts seven rope iterators, bulk-copies 4,096 records, reads 109,568 f32 operands, writes 97,280 f32 slots and compares 589,824 physical bytes, emitting 368,640. These are counts, not a fitted latency model or a claim that all writes are removable.

Dense physical output has a measured 368,640-byte emission floor for that Bitmap replay and a source-derived Omega(GF) Codec/physical-output floor when all G selected records change. The 589,824 compared bytes are current work, not necessarily a contract lower bound: exact dirty masks or a changed-range proof might narrow comparisons while preserving output. Same-length full replacement still has Omega(n) discovery unless an authored delta proves otherwise. Unsafe shaping windows, bidi/context and reflow can propagate; a short rope leaf does not imply local HarfRust or line work. No existing receipt gives an elapsed lower bound for 1,024 synchronous setters, a per-crossing coefficient, or the proportion of dense deficit due to GC/cache misses.

## What the variants actually tested

E2 retained the sparse/inspection advantages but missed dense parity. E3 chose whole-run shaping for admitted short broad edits and reset to full gather when all owners were on the frontier with at least half the old rows full-mask. It reused the same executor but **reset/repopulated** the packed gather, and the saved diagnostic recorded +4,469 allocations, +960 reallocations and +432,156 cumulative requested bytes per warm cycle versus E2. Its dense 512/1,024 p50 were 10.720/19.519 ms versus E2 9.816/18.497 in that separate, driftier eight-block series. E3 is a failed whole variant, not proof that every dense policy is slower. E4's cursor-local Codec input plan removed 12,288 f32 reads per forward/reverse Bitmap publication in its authenticated wave, without establishing a dense complete-operation win. E5 bypassed 175,104 hypothetical full-field writes using direct per-record locators but retained 184,320 locator bytes, indirect loads, admission and fallback; dense 512/1,024 p50 were 10.019/20.359 versus E2 9.385/18.514 in its own series. E6's row views removed per-operand slice construction but kept those locators and the same broad direct branch; its fixed matrix above was still slower in dense rows and used 1,377,716 more engine-only requested-live bytes and 46 more Wasm pages than E2. The E6 1,024 tail has no CPU profile. These are compatible preserved variants, but their measurements do not isolate combined effects or ban a future combination. They tested local substitutions and extra representation/admission costs, not a completed shared-storage dense overwrite.

## A feasible coherent hybrid, with exact boundary

Keep the current semantic owner and packed `CodecGatherWorkspace` as the sole authoritative prepared semantic and physical projection, respectively. Keep the current one executor, setter errors, query reuse, private wire ABI, batch/order and output format. The policy choice belongs **after semantic preparation, before gather/publication**, where the root already knows dirty owners, order, source counts, checkpoint status and Codec. It must choose by topology/invariants, not a benchmark percentage.

1. Reuse/no output: retain existing `prepare_reuse` when no semantic/placement dirt reaches physical output. Inspection reads the paragraph's prepared semantic columns and never forces root gather solely to answer a query.
2. Sparse or structural: retain current `append_retained_stream` dirty intervals, replacement correspondence and full fallback for mixed dirty/clean owners, changed source counts, decorations, reordered topology, selection changes and unfamiliar Codec fields. This preserves O(k log N + touched-record work + unavoidable packed-gap/topology/host widening) rather than charging every owner for a local edit.
3. Dense **same-source-count** candidate: when every active owner is on the dirty frontier, renderer order and decoration policy permit the existing packed layout, and a checked selection/record correspondence pass proves each source still maps to the same record ordinal, walk source and owner rows sequentially once. Update existing aligned f32/u32 lanes by indexed overwrite with the shared `gather_fields`/`update_fields` policy, then hand the unchanged `GatheredPlanInput` shape to the same Codec scalar/SIMD interpreter and ordered compiler. Update `GatherSource` identity/selection through the existing **transactional** rope walk: `update_ordered_ranges` rebuilds changed leaves and branches, preserving its failure guarantee. Do not allocate a per-record locator or full second set of lanes. Reuse capacity and one coarse full-range rope update; do not `begin`/clear/repopulate as E3 did. If exact eligibility fails before mutation, take the current retained/replacement/full path. Before changing packed lanes, prove that a later fallible callback cannot leak partial data into the accepted generation; otherwise stage minimal rollback state or reject the path. This path is O(N + G + GF) on dense inputs, plus the transactional rope copies, with O(1) additional per-record scratch if that rollback proof holds; its value is deletion of general retained gap/cursor/admission work, not avoidance of the required 9,216 physical records. The 10-unit-per-owner Bitmap shape remains in setter preparation; this path cannot repair a deficit dominated there.

This dense candidate has source-local building blocks (`GatherSource` already records selection; `AlignedField` has retained indexed writes; plan input already borrows the workspace; the rope has one ordered transactional traversal), but it is **not yet verified safe or faster**. In particular, a changed glyph can change raster selection, strike/resource/program, stable identity, placement slot or recordless status even with the same source count. The preflight must prove exact correspondence and preserve error order/custom Codec behavior. A too-expensive preflight can consume the saving; a post-write fallback must not leak half-mutated aligned lanes. A patch should share the current input-plan and record update helpers, remove the replaced dense general-walk calls, and report net source/size/memory rather than keep two similarly sized gather implementations. Do not add a new executor, speculative cache, second persistent physical format or per-row view objects. If this narrow dense mode cannot be expressed without duplicated Codec semantics or large extra storage, reject it and keep E4.

An implementor can bound the change to `state.rs` policy selection and `codec_gather.rs` traversal, leaving `ordered_plan.rs`'s one Codec pipeline intact. Form the canonical ordered owner frontier once from `pending_preparation_ids` plus `unpublished_preparation_ids`; admit dense mode only when it covers all active owners, current gather cache/revision is valid, there is no checkpoint/lifecycle/order/decorated topology change, and each owner's new positioned source count equals its prior range. Walk current `sources` and new positioned rows in lockstep to prove one-to-one source ordinal, selected/recordless cardinality, strike/resource selection key and compatible program/batch topology; an unknown or changing case returns the existing path before writing. This preflight is O(G), so its measured cost must be charged. Then run one complete transactional ordered range over `sources`, consuming each old `GatherSource` and updating stable ID/selection plus the existing selected-record ordinal's glyph, placement, mask and aligned fields through shared functions. The dense adapter needs no old-root clone solely for skip-prefix summaries because it has no clean gaps, but `update_ordered_ranges` still copies changed rope leaves/branches transactionally. Keep the existing output's single changed interval and `prepare_owned` comparison so byte-equal physical fields can still be suppressed. Fallible allocation and validation must be ahead of packed-lane mutation where possible; unresolved partial-write rollback is a blocker, not an excuse to weaken the last renderer-accepted generation or in-flight bytes. Reject the patch if its admission walk plus updates do not actually reduce owned work or if it requires a second full-buffer snapshot.

The best *unapproved* alternative is a root-wide canonical flat semantic/physical SoA with mutable ranges and a true batch of setter preparation. It could amortize allocation and crossings further, but a public synchronous setter still needs each revision independently queryable and failure-atomic; mutable flat ranges would copy trailing data or need segmented/persistent storage and revised ownership/in-flight rules. That crosses the expressly unapproved segmented-storage/public-API boundary. It should be considered only with a new design decision that explicitly accepts storage, memory, ABI and lifetime costs; no current evidence demonstrates it is needed for parity.

## Ranked next execution, with a stop condition

**First, close the smallest decision-changing attribution gap on exact active E4.** Do not time E6 again. Use the existing named-Wasm profile workflow on the 1,024-distinct-label no-read scene operation, with exact E4 archive/member and source authentication. Separate setter and publication ancestry, then inspect sampled JS request/decode, single-owner preflight/adoption, semantic preparation, gather/Codec and host frames where names resolve. This is a bounded locator, **not** an exact five-way cost decomposition: V8 sampling, inlining, GC and the native counter's different query demand leave some costs inseparable. Only if that capture is ambiguous, make one matched preparation-only capture; the executable method and cold-oracle limitation are in `e4-bounded-attribution-and-hybrid-implementation-brief.md`. This is not another full matrix or a substitute for one. The existing old/new profiled phase capture gives top-level phase movement; the new check asks whether a specific removable owner could plausibly close the observed E2/E6 dense gap. Avoid interpreting native shape events as elapsed time. The completed E7 observation, handoff SHA-256 `5f8407eb46fd05df95f1eaa118ffbe943ed56602d43ea3da650a588a197f5918`, finds all 2,048 actual-Bitmap old boundaries at offsets 5–9 present with `UNSAFE_TO_CONCAT` bit 2 and one `[0,10)` Prepared attempt per warm edit, with no retry. That retires shorter-suffix reuse for this cohort under current HarfRust boundary authority, subject to the separate hook-semantics review; it says nothing about elapsed lower bounds or the architecture as a whole.

**Second, choose exactly one implementation wave from the observed owner.** If repeated per-set transport/preflight/adoption dominates, fuse only the proven duplicate within `prepareParagraph`/`measure_paragraph_inner`, retaining its caller validation, inactive candidate and commit fence; count allocation and verify a prepared read after *every* setter, including rejected and subsequent explicit rebuild cases. If dense gather/general retained traversal dominates while semantic setter cost is near the required work floor, implement the dense same-count adapter above and delete the replaced dense walk, with E3/E5/E6 as controls. If shaping/flow dominates and authenticated old/new boundaries certify a shorter safe window, change only the existing shape-window selection with the same HarfRust authority and cold Unicode/ligature/RTL/fallback oracles. If no category exposes a removable pass large enough to plausibly close the measured gap, stop that wave and report the bounded evidence instead of trying several local switches.

**Third, acceptance for the one candidate.** Cold independent checks must compare semantic text, Unicode/bidi, run/cluster/positioned columns, IDs, measurement/glyph/caret/selection and independent `split` under sparse/dense/count-change/recordless/multifont/decoration/reorder/rollback and rejection/retry histories. Physical checks compare canonical buffers, patch ranges, bindings, batch order and accepted generations; CPU renderer recovery and archived-package browser pixels/editorial hero cover downstream behavior. Re-run the fixed seven families from one frozen shipping archive against same-machine main **and** published 0.1.0, E2 as a continuity control, with eight fresh single-call blocks, complete p50/p95, exact row coverage/exclusions and no favorable resampling. Report raw/gzip Wasm, package/peer/API/ABI, engine live/peak and whole-app physical/JS/GPU memory separately. Main/published nonregression across all seven families, without material unresolved tails, is parity; a favorable aggregate count or 5% display label is not. Only after parity permit up to three coherent boost waves. Stack `#292 → #301 → #302 → draft #310` stays held; no merge, release, tag or demo merge follows from this report.

